        "id": 1059882
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-06T23:25:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Would be nice for this to not have to deserialize/unparcel objects...  could it handle those by just copying the parcel data over?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 498,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 498,
        "endChar": 89
      },
      "revId": "600328e150292275cce234d23f3dd57163e08ca3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6a3fb644_662013fa",
        "filename": "core/java/android/os/BaseBundle.java",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 498,
      "author": {
        "id": 1133744
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-10T17:44:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The problem with doing that is that the deep copy has the following contract in its javadoc (despite the name): \"[...] Other types of objects (such as Parcelable or Serializable) are referenced as-is and not copied in any way.\"\n\nSo, if we did not deserialize them here (either by referencing the same LazyValue or by copying the LazyValue and its parcel), that would mean we\u0027d be breaking that contract because the objects returned by calling getParcelable(\u003csame key\u003e) in the original and in the copy would not be the same (since they\u0027d be lazily converted in its containing bundle but not in the other).\n\nWe could try to keep track of the maps that contain a certain LazyValue in LazyValue itself but I fear that might be a recipe for disaster :)\n\nThat\u0027s a public API hence my choice to stick to the more stable / minimal change approach. Let me know if you want to change that API (targetSdk gated?).",
      "parentUuid": "07756e7e_a59a7c45",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 498,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 498,
        "endChar": 89
      },
      "revId": "600328e150292275cce234d23f3dd57163e08ca3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
